id: design.redis-cache
kind: design
title: Redis as cache and coordination store
aliases: redis, redis cache, key value store
tags: caching, infrastructure, state
questions:
  - how would you use Redis in a system?
invariant: Redis is an in-memory, single-threaded-per-shard key/value store: excellent for derived, volatile state and coordination, dangerous as a system of record without persistence and a plan.
answer_30s: Use Redis for cache entries with TTLs, rate-limit counters, sessions, idempotency keys and simple locks -- data you can rebuild or lose -- and keep the durable record elsewhere. Redis is not the source of truth.
answer_2m: |
  What Redis is good at follows from its model: every command is atomic because
  execution is single-threaded per shard, which is why INCR, SETNX and Lua
  scripts are the building blocks for counters, locks and rate limiters. What to
  watch: latency spikes come from big KEYS or SCAN-like commands on the main
  thread, which is why you use separate databases or instances for cache and
  coordination, memory is finite so eviction policy (allkeys-lru vs noeviction)
  is a correctness decision, and persistence (RDB/AOF) reduces but does not
  remove the possibility of losing recent writes. A cache is not a queue: use a
  stream or a real broker for work that must not be lost, and treat every Redis
  read as "may be absent", never as "must be present".
when_to_use:
  - caching hot reads, counters, rate limits, sessions, short-lived locks, idempotency keys
avoid_when:
  - as the only store for data you cannot reconstruct; for durable queues; for large blobs
commands:
  - redis-cli --latency => is the instance's command latency healthy
  - redis-cli info memory => used_memory, evicted_keys and fragmentation in one place
  - redis-cli info stats => keyspace_hits/misses for the cache hit ratio (hits/(hits+misses))
  - redis-cli --bigkeys => which keys are large enough to cause latency spikes
failure_modes:
  - cache treated as authoritative, so an eviction or flush becomes data loss
  - the same instance serving cache and locks, so eviction breaks coordination
  - a single instance with no replica, so a restart is a cold-cache stampede on the database
follow_ups:
  - how do you avoid a cold-cache stampede after a Redis restart?
related:
  - design.cache-aside
  - design.cache-invalidation
  - design.distributed-locking
  - design.rate-limiting
  - recipe.redis-backed-service
sources:
  - https://redis.io/docs/manual/patterns/
